1. ARCHITECTURE OVERVIEW

The new architecture consists of the following services:

  a) API Gateway (Go, using Ocelot)
     Entry point for all external traffic. Handles authentication,
     rate limiting, request routing, and API versioning.

  b) User Service (Go)
     Manages user accounts, authentication, authorization, and
     profile data. Uses PostgreSQL with read replicas.

  c) Product Service (Go)
     Product catalog, inventory management, pricing. Uses
     PostgreSQL. Caches frequently accessed data in Redis.

  d) Order Service (Go)
     Order processing, payment integration, fulfillment
     orchestration. Uses PostgreSQL for orders and MongoDB
     for the event log.

  e) Notification Service (Go)
     Email, SMS, and push notification delivery. Uses RabbitMQ
     for queuing and templates stored in PostgreSQL.

  f) Search Service (Go, Elasticsearch)
     Full-text search across products and content. Maintains
     its own Elasticsearch cluster with daily reindexing.

  g) Analytics Service (Python, Apache Spark)
     Batch processing of user events and business metrics.
     Produces aggregated data consumed by the reporting dashboards.

2. SERVICE BOUNDARIES

Each service owns its data exclusively. No service may directly
access another service's database. Communication happens through
well-defined APIs (gRPC for internal, REST for external).

The bounded contexts are:

  * Identity & Access: User Service
  * Catalog Management: Product Service
  * Transaction Processing: Order Service
  * Customer Communication: Notification Service
  * Discovery: Search Service
  * Intelligence: Analytics Service

For domains that don't fit neatly into a single service, we use
the "Anti-Corruption Layer" pattern (see Evans, DDD) to translate
between bounded contexts.

3. MIGRATION STRATEGY

We will use the Strangler Fig pattern: gradually replace pieces
of the monolith with microservices, routing traffic to the new
services while the monolith continues to handle the rest.

Phase 1 — Infrastructure (Weeks 1-4)
  * Set up Kubernetes cluster (we're using GKE)
  * Deploy service mesh (Istio)
  * Set up CI/CD pipelines with GitHub Actions
  * Implement centralized logging (Loki + Grafana)
  * Deploy Redis and RabbitMQ clusters

Phase 2 — Extract User Service (Weeks 5-8)
  * Extract authentication and user management from monolith
  * Implement OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect
  * Migrate user data with zero downtime
  * All new sign-ups go through User Service
  * Legacy auth still works in monolith (dual-write)

Phase 3 — Extract Product Service (Weeks 9-12)
  * Extract product catalog and inventory management
  * Implement full-text search in Elasticsearch
  * Product admin UI moved to new service
  * Read traffic gradually shifted (20% → 50% → 100%)

Phase 4 — Extract Order Service (Weeks 13-18)
  * This is the most complex migration
  * Order processing, payment, fulfillment
  * Requires transactional outbox pattern for consistency
  * Dual-write with verification for at least 2 weeks

Phase 5 — Extract Remaining Services (Weeks 19-24)
  * Notification Service
  * Analytics Service (this can be done in parallel)
  * Decommission monolith

4. DATA MANAGEMENT

This is the hardest part. Here are the key decisions:

Database per Service
  Each service gets its own database instance. No shared databases.

Data Synchronization
  During migration, we use Change Data Capture (CDC) via Debezium
  to keep the monolith and new services in sync.

  We push events to Kafka topics:
    * user.events
    * product.events
    * order.events

  Each service consumes the events relevant to it.

Eventual Consistency
  We accept eventual consistency for cross-service queries.
  The API gateway handles saga orchestration where needed.

  For critical operations (like order placement), we use a
  distributed saga pattern with compensating transactions.

CQRS
  Read models are maintained separately from write models.
  The Search Service maintains its own denormalized data
  for efficient querying.

5. COMMUNICATION PATTERNS

Synchronous (gRPC):
  * Service-to-service queries
  * Authentication/authorization checks
  * Real-time inventory lookups

Asynchronous (RabbitMQ):
  * Event notifications
  * Long-running task orchestration
  * Cross-service data replication

We use the following message patterns:

  * Command: Direct request to a specific service
  * Event: Broadcast to all interested services
  * Saga: Multi-step transaction with compensation

Retry and Circuit Breaker:
  * All gRPC calls use exponential backoff with jitter
  * Circuit breaker opens after 5 failures in 30 seconds
  * Half-open state after 15 seconds

6. OBSERVABILITY

Every service must expose:

  * Metrics: Prometheus endpoints (/metrics)
  * Tracing: OpenTelemetry distributed tracing
  * Logging: Structured JSON logs to stdout

We have three golden signals:

  Latency: P50 < 50ms, P95 < 200ms, P99 < 500ms
  Traffic: Requests per second per endpoint
  Errors: Error rate per status code family
  Saturation: CPU, memory, connections per service

Grafana dashboards are organized by service, with a top-level
"Service Overview" dashboard showing key metrics for all services.

Alerts are configured in AlertManager:
  * Page: P99 latency > 1s for 5 minutes
  * Page: Error rate > 5% for 2 minutes
  * Warning: CPU > 80% for 10 minutes
  * Warning: Disk usage > 85%

7. DEPLOYMENT

All services are containerized and deployed to Kubernetes.

Deployment Strategy:
  * Blue-green deployment for all services
  * Canary releases for high-risk changes (5% → 25% → 100%)
  * Feature flags for gradual rollout

CI/CD Pipeline:
  * PR merged → Unit tests → Build image → Push to registry
  * Deploy to staging → Integration tests → Smoke tests
  * Deploy to production (blue-green)

Kubernetes Configuration:
  * Horizontal Pod Autoscaler based on CPU/memory
  * Pod Disruption Budget: min 2 available
  * Resource requests and limits for all containers
  * Network policies to restrict inter-service traffic

8. TESTING STRATEGY

Testing Pyramid for each service:

  * Unit Tests: 70% coverage minimum
    Test business logic in isolation with mocked dependencies.

  * Integration Tests: 20%
    Test database interactions, message queue producers/consumers.

  * Contract Tests: 5%
    Use Pact for consumer-driven contract tests between services.

  * End-to-End Tests: 5%
    Test critical user journeys across all services.

We also run:
  * Chaos engineering experiments (weekly)
  * Load tests (monthly, targeting 3x peak traffic)
  * Security scans (daily, integrated into CI)

9. ROLLBACK PLAN

Every deployment must have a corresponding rollback plan.

Rollback triggers:
  * Error rate increase > 3%
  * P99 latency increase > 50%
  * Any P0 incident opened within 30 minutes of deployment

Rollback procedure:
  1. Identify the affected service and scope
  2. If feature flagged: disable the feature flag
  3. If canary: shift traffic back to previous version
  4. If blue-green: switch active environment
  5. Monitor for 30 minutes after rollback
  6. Open incident ticket with findings

Database rollbacks:
  * All schema migrations are forward-only
  * Additive changes only (no destructive migrations)
  * Rollback means deploying previous application version
  * Never roll back a database migration

10. TIMELINE & MILESTONES

  Month 1-2: Foundation (Infrastructure + CI/CD)
  Month 3-4: User Service migration complete
  Month 5-6: Product Service migration complete
  Month 7-9: Order Service migration complete
  Month 10-11: Notification + Analytics migrated
  Month 12: Monolith decommissioned
  
Key milestones:
  M1 (Week 4): Green cluster with all infra operational
  M2 (Week 8): User Service handles 100% of auth traffic
  M3 (Week 12): Product Service handles 100% of catalog
  M4 (Week 18): Order Service handles 100% of orders
  M5 (Week 24): All services migrated, monolith shutdown

